          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
          %p
            The following are a sequence of examples all of the form that an abstractly constructed topological space is homeomorphic to a certain subspace of a Euclidean space. These examples are going to be useful in further developments below, for example in the
            %a{:href => "#BorelHeineProof"} proof below
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heine-Borel+theorem"} Heine-Borel theorem
            (prop.
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#BorelHeine"}
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product+topological+space"} Products
                  of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intervals"} intervals
                  are homeomorphic to
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cube"} hypercubes
                (example
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ClosedIntervalsProduct"}
            %li
              %p
                %em
                  The
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+interval"} closed interval
                  glued at its endpoints is homeomorphic to the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle"} circle
                (example
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#HomeomorphismBetweenTopologicalAndCombinatorialCircle"}
            %li
              %p
                %em
                  The
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cylinder"} cylinder
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M%C3%B6bius+strip"} Möbius strip
                  and the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/torus"} torus
                  are all homeomorphic to
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient+spaces"} quotients
                  of the square
