          .proof
            %h6#proof_24 Proof
            %p
              First we observe that every continuous function
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi X
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \colon X \longrightarrow Y
              into a
              = succeed "-topological" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi T
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_n
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
              factors uniquely, via
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi t
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t_n(X)
              through a continuous function
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde f
              (this makes use of the “
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+property"}> universal property
              ” of the quotient topology, which we dwell on a bit more below in example
              = succeed "):" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#UniversalPropertyOfQuotientSpace"}
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo =
                    %mover
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                    %mo ∘
                    %msub
                      %mi t
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    f = \tilde f \circ t_n(X)
            %p
              Clearly this continuous function
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde f
              is unique if it exists, because its underlying function of sets must be given by
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo ↦
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\tilde f \colon [x] \mapsto f(x)
                    \\,.
            %p
              First observe that this is indeed well defined as a function of underlying sets. To that end, factor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              through its
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/image"} image
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(X)
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi X
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    f \;\colon\; X \longrightarrow f(X) \hookrightarrow Y
            %p
              equipped with its
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+subspace"} subspace topology
              as a subspace of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
              (example
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ImageFactorization"}
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#TiSubspaces"}
              also the image
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(X)
              is a
              = succeed "-topological" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi T
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_n
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
              is. This means that if two elements
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_1, x_2 \in X
              have the same equivalence class, then, by definition of the equivalence relation, they have the same image under
              %em all
              continuous surjective functions into a
              = succeed "-space," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi T
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_n
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi X
                      %mover
                        %mo ⟶
                        %mtext surjective
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ↪
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \colon X \overset{\text{surjective}}{\longrightarrow} f(X) \hookrightarrow Y
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable{:columnalign => "right left right left right left right left right left", :columnspacing => "0em", :displaystyle => "true"}
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                            %msub
                              %mi x
                              %mn 1
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                            %mo =
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                            %msub
                              %mi x
                              %mn 2
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mo ⇔
                            %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %msub
                              %mi x
                              %mn 1
                            %mo ∼
                            %msub
                              %mi x
                              %mn 2
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ⇒
                            %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi f
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %msub
                              %mi x
                              %mn 1
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo =
                            %mi f
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %msub
                              %mi x
                              %mn 2
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                            %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\begin{aligned}
                    ( [x_1] = [x_2]) &amp; \Leftrightarrow\, (x_1 \sim x_2)
                    \\\
                    \&amp; \Rightarrow\, ( f(x_1) = f(x_2) )
                    \\,.
                    \\end{aligned}
            %p
              This shows that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde f
              is well defined as a function between sets.
            %p
              To see that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde f
              is also continuous, consider
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \in Y
              an open subset. We need to show that the pre-image
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mover
                        %mi f
