                          %mi i
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi X
                          %mi i
                        %mo ,
                        %msub
                          %mi τ
                          %mi i
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \underset{\longleftarrow}{\lim}_i (X_i, \tau_i)
                  according to prop.
                  %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#DescriptionOfLimitsAndColimitsInTop"}
                  again a Hausdorff space, but it also satisfies its universal property with respect to the category of Hausdorff spaces;
              %li
                %p
                  not only is the reflection
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi T
                          %mn 2
                        %mrow
                          %mo (
                          %msub
                            %munder
                              %mi lim
                              %mo ⟶
                            %mi i
                          %msub
                            %mi X
                            %mi i
                          %mo )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_2 \left( \underset{\longrightarrow}{\lim}_i X_i \right)
                  of the colimit as topological spaces a Hausdorff space (while the colimit as topological spaces in general is not), but this reflection does satisfy the universal property of a colimit with respect to the category of Hausdorff spaces.
          .proof
            %h6#proof_36 Proof
            %p First to see that right/left adjoint functors preserve limits/colimits: We discuss the case of the right adjoint functor preserving limits. The other case is directly analogous (just reverse the direction of all arrows).
            %p
              So let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %munder
                        %mi lim
                        %mo ⟵
                      %mi i
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \underset{\longleftarrow}{\lim}_i X_i
              be the limit over some diagram
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mrow
                          %mo {
                          %msub
                            %mi X
                            %mi i
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi f
                                %mi α
                          %msub
                            %mi X
                            %mi j
                          %mo }
                        %mrow
                          %mi i
                          %mo ,
                          %mi j
                          %mo ∈
                          %mi I
                          %mo ,
                          %mi α
                          %mo ∈
                          %msub
                            %mi I
                            %mrow
                              %mi i
                              %mo ,
                              %mi j
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \left\{ X_i \overset{f_\alpha}{\to} X_j\right\}_{i,j \in I, \alpha \in I_{i,j}}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
              into it. Using prop.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#HomFunctorPreservesLimits"}
              this yields
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable{:columnalign => "right left right left right left right left right left", :columnspacing => "0em", :displaystyle => "true"}
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi Hom
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi Y
                            %mo ,
                            %mi R
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %msub
                              %munder
                                %mi lim
                                %mo ⟵
                              %mi i
                            %msub
                              %mi X
                              %mi i
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mo ≃
                            %mi Hom
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi L
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi Y
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
