          %p
            We give a proof of the finitary case of the Tychonoff theorem using the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed-projection+characterization+of+compactness"} closed-projection characterization of compactness
            from prop.
            = succeed "." do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ClosedProjectionCharacterizationOfCompactness"}
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "closed-projection+characterization+of+compactness#TychonoffTheorem"} here
          .proof
            %h6#proof_of_the_finitary_case Proof of the finitary case
            %p
              By prop.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ClosedProjectionCharacterizationOfCompactness"}
              it is sufficient to show that for every topological space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Y
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi τ
                      %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (Y,\tau_Y)
              then the projection
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mi Y
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Y
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi τ
                      %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ×
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %munder
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                        %mrow
                          %mi i
                          %mo ∈
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                          %mn 1
                          %mo ,
                          %mi ⋯
                          %mo ,
                          %mi n
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mi i
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi τ
                        %mi i
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo )
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Y
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi τ
                      %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\pi_Y
                    \\;\colon\;
                    (Y, \tau_Y)
                    \\times
                    \\left(
                    \\underset{i \in \{1, \cdots, n\}}{\prod} (X_i, \tau_i)
                    \\right)
                    \\longrightarrow
                    (Y, \tau_Y)
            %p
              is a closed map. We proceed by
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/induction"} induction
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n = 0
              the statement is obvious. Suppose it has been proven for some
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi ℕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \in \mathbb{N}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n+1
              factors is the composite of two consecutive projections
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mi Y
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi Y
                    %mo ×
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %munder
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                        %mrow
                          %mi i
                          %mo ∈
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                          %mn 1
                          %mo ,
                          %mi ⋯
                          %mo ,
                          %mi n
                          %mo +
                          %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mi i
                      %mo )
                    %mo =
                    %mi Y
                    %mo ×
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %munder
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                        %mrow
                          %mi i
                          %mo ∈
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                          %mn 1
                          %mo ,
                          %mi ⋯
                          %mo ,
                          %mi n
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mi i
                      %mo )
                    %mo ×
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mi Y
                    %mo ×
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %munder
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                        %mrow
                          %mi i
                          %mo ∈
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                          %mn 1
                          %mo ,
                          %mi ⋯
                          %mo ,
                          %mo ,
                          %mi n
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mi i
                      %mo )
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mi Y
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\pi_Y
                    \\;\colon\;
                    Y \times \left(\underset{i \in \{1, \cdots, n + 1\} }{\prod} X_i \right)
                    \=
                    Y \times \left(\underset{i \in \{1, \cdots, n\} }{\prod} X_i \right) \times X_{n+1}
                    \\longrightarrow
                    Y \times \left(\underset{i \in \{1, \cdots, ,n\} }{\prod} X_i \right)
                    \\longrightarrow
                    Y
                    \\,.
            %p
              By prop.
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ClosedProjectionCharacterizationOfCompactness"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi τ
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X_{n+1}, \tau_{n+1})
              is compact by the assumption of the proposition, and similarly the second is closed by induction assumption. Hence the composite is a closed map.
          %p
            Of course we also want to claim that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sequentially+compact+metric+spaces"} sequentially compact metric spaces
            (def.
            = succeed ")" do
