          %h3#transition_functions Transition functions
          %p
            We discuss how topological vector bundles are equivalently given by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocycles"} cocycles
            (def.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#CocycleCech"}
            below) in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cech+cohomology"} Cech cohomology
            (def.
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#CohomologyCech"}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/transition+functions"} transition functions
            (def.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#TransitionFunctions"}
            below). This allows to make precise the intuition that vector bundles are precisely the result of “continuously gluing” trivial vector bundles onto each other“ (prop.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#TopologicalVectorBundlesCharacterizedByCechCohomology"}
            below).
          %p
            This gives a “local-to-global principle” for constructions on vector bundles. For instance it allows to easily obtain concepts of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/direct+sum+of+vector+bundles"} direct sum of vector bundles
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product+of+vector+bundles"} tensor product of vector bundles
            (def.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#DirectSumOfTopologicalVectorBundlesViaTransitionFunctions"}
            and def.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#TensorProductOfVectorBundles"}
            below) by applying the usual operations from linear algebra on a local trivialization and then re-gluing the result via the combined transition functions.
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
